ut-write-containment-hardening/research/worktree-symlinks\" \"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/iterations\" 2>/dev/null; echo \"=== 045 root docs ===\"; ls \"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research\" | head; wc -c \"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/alternatives/synthesis.md\" \"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/decision-record.md\"","time":{"start":1789366766107,"end":1789366766109}},"id":"prt_09e91f2c1001sWepGtodwrswtp","sessionID":"ses_f6176138cffeODZD3dhSK78dPo","messageID":"msg_09e90afde001vCH8hUzN4zSfRg"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1789366767399,"sessionID":"ses_f6176138cffeODZD3dhSK78dPo","part":{"id":"prt_09e920f24001dGP0qR5p3K70t3","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"e6a67ce4d063ae8df242a5d6242277d7c64cb8f3","messageID":"msg_09e90afde001vCH8hUzN4zSfRg","sessionID":"ses_f6176138cffeODZD3dhSK78dPo","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":115019,"input":6774,"output":337,"reasoning":4868,"cache":{"write":0,"read":103040}},"cost":0.004473362}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1789366774971,"sessionID":"ses_f6176138cffeODZD3dhSK78dPo","part":{"id":"prt_09e922cb6001gYFpF6nUKXT5Fn","messageID":"msg_09e921330001ibTk53Iex7eRnh","sessionID":"ses_f6176138cffeODZD3dhSK78dPo","snapshot":"e6a67ce4d063ae8df242a5d6242277d7c64cb8f3","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789366794022,"sessionID":"ses_f6176138cffeODZD3dhSK78dPo","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-993f32af95f05089","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/alternatives/synthesis.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/alternatives/synthesis.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Alternatives to full per-lane worktrees: synthesis\n2: \n3: **Question.** Per-lane git worktrees give exact write attribution at roughly 1.6 GB of checkout and about 22 s of setup per lane. Can the same parallel fan-out and the same attribution guarantee be had for a fraction of that?\n4: \n5: **Lanes.** GPT-5.6 LUNA at max via Codex, SWE-2 max via Devin, DeepSeek V4 Flash max via Devin. Three iterations each, no early convergence, run against this repository. Every load-bearing number below was re-measured by the orchestrator rather than taken from a lane.\n6: \n7: ---\n8: \n9: ## The short answer\n10: \n11: Yes. The cost is tree materialization, not git objects, and the tree is dominated by content a lane never reads. A **sparse-cone worktree** keeps the same repository identity and every runner semantic while checking out only what the lane needs, cutting the disk cost by 62% at family granularity and 85% at packet granularity, on this repository, measured. All three lanes reached this independently.\n12: \n13: A second step, **clonefile materialization inside a registered `--no-checkout` worktree**, can push disk near zero on APFS, which this volume is. It is platform-gated and its setup time is unmeasured.\n14: \n15: Nothing else survives. Shallow and reference clones attack the wrong axis; partial and blobless modes have nothing to fetch lazily from a repo that already holds every blob locally; single-checkout write redirection and unregistered copy-on-write directories both lose git identity, which is what attribution, the churn detector and relocation all rest on.\n16: \n17: ---\n18: \n19: ## What the cost actually is\n20: \n21: | | Measured |\n22: |---|---|\n23: | Tracked tree | 1,425 MB across 82,376 files |\n24: | of which `specs/` | 1,205 MB, 85% |\n25: | of which `.opencode/` | 216 MB |\n26: | Object store, shared, not per lane | 2.06 GiB in pack |\n27: | `.opencode` + the deep-loop spec family | 546 MB, a 62% cut |\n28: | `.opencode` + one packet | 216 MB, an 85% cut |\n29: \n30: The object store is already shared by every worktree. What a lane pays for is the checkout, and 85% of the checkout is other packets' documents.\n31: \n32: ---\n33: \n34: ## Mechanism by mechanism\n35: \n36: | Mechanism | Setup | Disk | Attribution exact | Relocation survives | Verdict |\n37: |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n38: | Full worktree, today | ~22 s | 1.6 GB | yes | yes | baseline |\n39: | **Sparse-cone worktree** | falls with the cone | 216 to 546 MB | yes, same repo identity | yes | **adopt first** |\n40: | Registered `--no-checkout` worktree + clonefile | unmeasured, clonefile is metadata-only | near zero on APFS | yes, the worktree is registered | yes | second step, APFS only |\n41: | Shallow clone | full tree still materializes | 1.6 GB | adds a repo boundary the sweep cannot see | n/a | ruled out, all three lanes |\n42: | Reference or shared clone | objects already shared | no gain | same boundary problem | n/a | ruled out, all three lanes |\n43: | Partial or blobless | needs a transport; a local worktree has every blob already | no gain | n/a | n/a | ruled out; LUNA's variant is a no-op here |\n44: | Single checkout, per-lane write redirection | none | none | no: the runner's own `git -C` calls strip the redirectors | n/a | ruled out, all three lanes; scored 3/10 in an earlier decision |\n45: | Unregistered CoW or overlay directory | fast | near zero | no: not a git working tree, so status, the detector and the sweep cannot see it | no: move and repair act on registered worktrees only | ruled out, all three lanes |\n46: \n47: The one place the lanes disagreed was copy-on-write, and it resolves on a single distinction. Deepseek ruled out CoW *as a replacement for* a worktree, which is correct. LUNA and SWE-2 proposed clonefile *inside* a registered worktree created with `--no-checkout`: git registers the tree first, so identity holds, and clonefile fills it instead of checkout. LUNA's own rule-outs, unregistered CoW directories and shared overlay layers, draw exactly the line that makes SWE-2's variant safe.\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## What breaks, and what does not\n52: \n53: Sparse-cone worktrees change nothing the packet built. The lease sits at the tree root, which every cone includes. The six shared dependency roots and the shared package's build all live under `.opencode/`, which every cone includes, so split-link provisioning and the relative self-links work unchanged, and relocation survives because those links are relative. The churn detector and containment compare against the same repo, so they stay exact.\n54: \n55: Two things need care:\n56: \n57: - **The cone is a new runner concept.** It is the union of `.opencode/`, the target packet or its family, any other spec path the dispatch names, and the seed path list, so seeded bytes never surface as untracked noise. Cone mode is include-only per directory.\n58: - **Sparse mode flips `extensions.worktreeConfig`, and that is repository-wide.** It is unset today. Git 2.50.1 here is fine, but 30 other worktrees would inherit the extension the moment any lane enabled it, and an older git refuses such a repository. Nothing in the hooks, sk-git scripts, or the provisioners reads `core.sparseCheckout` or assumes the old layout, verified, but a minimum git version must be enforced before the first sparse lane runs.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## What is still unmeasured\n63: \n64: Setup time under a sparse cone. Every lane estimated it falls with the materialization ratio and every lane said the estimate needs the packet's stub-executor protocol to confirm. Taking that measurement means enabling the repository-wide extension on a live checkout with 30 worktrees and other sessions active, so it was deliberately not taken here. It belongs in a throwaway clone, with the operator's say-so.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## Findings against the current mechanism, from SWE-2\n69: \n70: Two defects independent of this decision, both worth their own packet:\n71: \n72: 1. **A lane can write through a shared dependency root into the main checkout.** The vendored trees are wholesale-linked by design, so a write into one lands in main and escapes both the tree-rooted guard and the checkout watch.\n73: 2. **The churn detector has no cumulative arm.** It fires on a burst above the threshold within one heartbeat window. A neighbour dirtying one or two files per window never trips it, however long that goes on.\n74: \n75: ---\n76: \n77: ## Defects in the runner, surfaced by this run\n78: \n79: 1. **LUNA's lane was rejected with its work complete.** Its state log held each iteration record twice: once with millisecond timestamps from the append gateway, once with round, backdated ones the model wrote directly, against the agent contract's instruction never to write that file. The validator counts records and rejects; it should deduplicate by iteration and prefer the gateway's. This is the recurrence the packet's limitation four predicted and asked to keep evidence for; the lineage directory is kept.\n80: 2. **SWE-2's lane was accepted with nothing registered.** Its findings are numbered list items, and the lineage reducer's list extractor takes only bullets, so its registry stayed empty. The merge's reconstruction path reads both forms, which is how its 27 findings reached the merged registry, but a fulfilled lane with three deltas and an empty registry should be flagged, not passed.\n81: \n82: Both lanes also fabricated round timestamps in their own state records; the runner detected it for deepseek and the pattern is visible in LUNA's. Lane self-reported timing should not be cited.\n83: \n84: ---\n85: \n86: ## Recommendation\n87: \n88: 1. Implement sparse-cone worktrees behind the existing `--worktrees` opt-in, with cone computation as described and a git-version gate. Measure setup time in a throwaway clone first.\n89: 2. Prototype clonefile materialization as an APFS-only refinement once the sparse cone is in and measured.\n90: 3. File the two current-mechanism defects and the two runner defects as their own work.\n91: 4. Leave the default off until the sparse cone's measured cost is known.\n\n(End of file - total 91 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Alternatives to full per-lane worktrees: synthesis\n\n**Question.** Per-lane git worktrees give exact write attribution at roughly 1.6 GB of checkout and about 22 s of setup per lane. Can the same parallel fan-out and the same attribution guarantee be had for a fraction of that?\n\n**Lanes.** GPT-5.6 LUNA at max via Codex, SWE-2 max via Devin, DeepSeek V4 Flash max via Devin. Three iterations each, no early convergence, run against this repository. Every load-bearing number below was re-measured by the orchestrator rather than taken from a lane.\n\n---\n\n## The short answer\n\nYes. The cost is tree materialization, not git objects, and the tree is dominated by content a lane never reads. A **sparse-cone worktree** keeps the same repository identity and every runner semantic while checking out only what the lane needs, cutting the disk cost by 62% at family granularity and 85% at packet granularity, on this repository, measured. All three lanes reached this independently.\n\nA second step, **clonefile materialization inside a registered `--no-checkout` worktree**, can push disk near zero on APFS, which this volume is. It is platform-gated and its setup time is unmeasured.\n\nNothing else survives. Shallow and reference clones attack the wrong axis; partial and blobless modes have nothing to fetch lazily from a repo that already holds every blob locally; single-checkout write redirection and unregistered copy-on-write directories both lose git identity, which is what attribution, the churn detector and relocation all rest on.\n\n---\n\n## What the cost actually is\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/alternatives/synthesis.md","text":"# Alternatives to full per-lane worktrees: synthesis\n\n**Question.** Per-lane git worktrees give exact write attribution at roughly 1.6 GB of checkout and about 22 s of setup per lane. Can the same parallel fan-out and the same attribution guarantee be had for a fraction of that?\n\n**Lanes.** GPT-5.6 LUNA at max via Codex, SWE-2 max via Devin, DeepSeek V4 Flash max via Devin. Three iterations each, no early convergence, run against this repository. Every load-bearing number below was re-measured by the orchestrator rather than taken from a lane.\n\n---\n\n## The short answer\n\nYes. The cost is tree materialization, not git objects, and the tree is dominated by content a lane never reads. A **sparse-cone worktree** keeps the same repository identity and every runner semantic while checking out only what the lane needs, cutting the disk cost by 62% at family granularity and 85% at packet granularity, on this repository, measured. All three lanes reached this independently.\n\nA second step, **clonefile materialization inside a registered `--no-checkout` worktree**, can push disk near zero on APFS, which this volume is. It is platform-gated and its setup time is unmeasured.\n\nNothing else survives. Shallow and reference clones attack the wrong axis; partial and blobless modes have nothing to fetch lazily from a repo that already holds every blob locally; single-checkout write redirection and unregistered copy-on-write directories both lose git identity, which is what attribution, the churn detector and relocation all rest on.\n\n---\n\n## What the cost actually is\n\n| | Measured |\n|---|---|\n| Tracked tree | 1,425 MB across 82,376 files |\n| of which `specs/` | 1,205 MB, 85% |\n| of which `.opencode/` | 216 MB |\n| Object store, shared, not per lane | 2.06 GiB in pack |\n| `.opencode` + the deep-loop spec family | 546 MB, a 62% cut |\n| `.opencode` + one packet | 216 MB, an 85% cut |\n\nThe object store is already shared by every worktree. What a lane pays for is the checkout, and 85% of the checkout is other packets' documents.\n\n---\n\n## Mechanism by mechanism\n\n| Mechanism | Setup | Disk | Attribution exact | Relocation survives | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Full worktree, today | ~22 s | 1.6 GB | yes | yes | baseline |\n| **Sparse-cone worktree** | falls with the cone | 216 to 546 MB | yes, same repo identity | yes | **adopt first** |\n| Registered `--no-checkout` worktree + clonefile | unmeasured, clonefile is metadata-only | near zero on APFS | yes, the worktree is registered | yes | second step, APFS only |\n| Shallow clone | full tree still materializes | 1.6 GB | adds a repo boundary the sweep cannot see | n/a | ruled out, all three lanes |\n| Reference or shared clone | objects already shared | no gain | same boundary problem | n/a | ruled out, all three lanes |\n| Partial or blobless | needs a transport; a local worktree has every blob already | no gain | n/a | n/a | ruled out; LUNA's variant is a no-op here |\n| Single checkout, per-lane write redirection | none | none | no: the runner's own `git -C` calls strip the redirectors | n/a | ruled out, all three lanes; scored 3/10 in an earlier decision |\n| Unregistered CoW or overlay directory | fast | near zero | no: not a git working tree, so status, the detector and the sweep cannot see it | no: move and repair act on registered worktrees only | ruled out, all three lanes |\n\nThe one place the lanes disagreed was copy-on-write, and it resolves on a single distinction. Deepseek ruled out CoW *as a replacement for* a worktree, which is correct. LUNA and SWE-2 proposed clonefile *inside* a registered worktree created with `--no-checkout`: git registers the tree first, so identity holds, and clonefile fills it instead of checkout. LUNA's own rule-outs, unregistered CoW directories and shared overlay layers, draw exactly the line that makes SWE-2's variant safe.\n\n---\n\n## What breaks, and what does not\n\nSparse-cone worktrees change nothing the packet built. The lease sits at the tree root, which every cone includes. The six shared dependency roots and the shared package's build all live under `.opencode/`, which every cone includes, so split-link provisioning and the relative self-links work unchanged, and relocation survives because those links are relative. The churn detector and containment compare against the same repo, so they stay exact.\n\nTwo things need care:\n\n- **The cone is a new runner concept.** It is the union of `.opencode/`, the target packet or its family, any other spec path the dispatch names, and the seed path list, so seeded bytes never surface as untracked noise. Cone mode is include-only per directory.\n- **Sparse mode flips `extensions.worktreeConfig`, and that is repository-wide.** It is unset today. Git 2.50.1 here is fine, but 30 other worktrees would inherit the extension the moment any lane enabled it, and an older git refuses such a repository. Nothing in the hooks, sk-git scripts, or the provisioners reads `core.sparseCheckout` or assumes the old layout, verified, but a minimum git version must be enforced before the first sparse lane runs.\n\n---\n\n## What is still unmeasured\n\nSetup time under a sparse cone. Every lane estimated it falls with the materialization ratio and every lane said the estimate needs the packet's stub-executor protocol to confirm. Taking that measurement means enabling the repository-wide extension on a live checkout with 30 worktrees and other sessions active, so it was deliberately not taken here. It belongs in a throwaway clone, with the operator's say-so.\n\n---\n\n## Findings against the current mechanism, from SWE-2\n\nTwo defects independent of this decision, both worth their own packet:\n\n1. **A lane can write through a shared dependency root into the main checkout.** The vendored trees are wholesale-linked by design, so a write into one lands in main and escapes both the tree-rooted guard and the checkout watch.\n2. **The churn detector has no cumulative arm.** It fires on a burst above the threshold within one heartbeat window. A neighbour dirtying one or two files per window never trips it, however long that goes on.\n\n---\n\n## Defects in the runner, surfaced by this run\n\n1. **LUNA's lane was rejected with its work complete.** Its state log held each iteration record twice: once with millisecond timestamps from the append gateway, once with round, backdated ones the model wrote directly, against the agent contract's instruction never to write that file. The validator counts records and rejects; it should deduplicate by iteration and prefer the gateway's. This is the recurrence the packet's limitation four predicted and asked to keep evidence for; the lineage directory is kept.\n2. **SWE-2's lane was accepted with nothing registered.** Its findings are numbered list items, and the lineage reducer's list extractor takes only bullets, so its registry stayed empty. The merge's reconstruction path reads both forms, which is how its 27 findings reached the merged registry, but a fulfilled lane with three deltas and an empty registry should be flagged, not passed.\n\nBoth lanes also fabricated round timestamps in their own state records; the runner detected it for deepseek and the pattern is visible in LUNA's. Lane self-reported timing should not be cited.\n\n---\n\n## Recommendation\n\n1. Implement sparse-cone worktrees behind the existing `--worktrees` opt-in, with cone computation as described and a git-version gate. Measure setup time in a throwaway clone first.\n2. Prototype clonefile materialization as an APFS-only refinement once the sparse cone is in and measured.\n3. File the two current-mechanism defects and the two runner defects as their own work.\n4. Leave the default off until the sparse cone's measured cost is known.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":91,"totalLines":91,"truncated":false}},"title":"specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/alternatives/synthesis.md","time":{"start":1789366794015,"end":1789366794020}},"id":"prt_09e9274ba001sGjPt3xDpGfBXx","sessionID":"ses_f6176138cffeODZD3dhSK78dPo","messageID":"msg_09e921330001ibTk53Iex7eRnh"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1789366796768,"sessionID":"ses_f6176138cffeODZD3dhSK78dPo","part":{"id":"prt_09e9272d0001oLQOywp3NfbO9c","messageID":"msg_09e921330001ibTk53Iex7eRnh","sessionID":"ses_f6176138cffeODZD3dhSK78dPo","type":"text","text":"Phase clarity achieved. Now the mechanics are mapped; next I read the prior lanes' synthesis (my challenge target) and the packet's measurements.","time":{"start":1789366792912,"end":1789366796767}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789366796940,"sessionID":"ses_f6176138cffeODZD3dhSK78dPo","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-83357c3d82b0d406","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"P=\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening\"; wc -c \"$P/research/fanout-attribution.md\" \"$P/research/synthesis.md\" \"$P/research/worktree-symlinks/synthesis.md\" \"$P/research/open-questions/synthesis.md\" \"$P/research/orchestration-summary.json\"; echo \"=== measurements: 1.6 GB / 22 s in packet ===\"; grep -rn \"1\\.6\" --include='*.md' \"$P\" | head -12; grep -rn \"22 *s\\|22-sec\\|22 second\\|~22\" --include='*.md' \"$P\" | head -8; echo \"=== decision-record worktree/ADR-003 ===\"; grep -n \"ADR-003\\|ADR-005\\|worktree\" \"$P/decision-record.md\" | head -25"},"output":"     435 /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/fanout-attribution.md\n    6116 /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/synthesis.md\n    8591 /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/worktree-symlinks/synthesis.md\n    4568 /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/open-questions/synthesis.md\n    1922 /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/orchestration-summary.json\n   21632 total\n=== measurements: 1.6 GB / 22 s in packet ===\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/synthesis-v1.md:24:The packet records 16.5 seconds without worktrees versus 149.7 seconds with worktrees for six lanes at concurrency 3, and 1.6 GB of checked-out files per tree with a shared object store [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:98-110]. Alternative setup and disk deltas remain unmeasured.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/research.md:14:The packet’s observed baseline is 16.5 seconds for six lanes without worktrees versus 149.7 seconds with worktrees at concurrency 3, and 1.6 GB of checked-out files per tree; the object store is shared [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:98-110]. No alternative was benchmarked here, so every fractional cost claim is derived or inferred. The terminal loop stop is `maxIterationsReached` after three iterations; convergence mode was off.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/research.md:32:The packet reports 16.5 seconds without worktrees and 149.7 seconds with worktrees for six lanes at concurrency 3, a 133.2-second wall-time difference in that run. It reports 1.6 GB of checked-out files per tree and roughly 9.6 GB for six trees, while the Git object store is shared [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:98-110; specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/handover.md:39-44]. The packet’s decision record describes provisioning as the hot-path cost and worktrees as the structural isolation fix [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/decision-record.md:391-425].\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/research.md:58:| Sparse checkout in a registered worktree | `git worktree add` plus sparse configuration; delta UNKNOWN | Selected tracked paths can be below the measured 1.6 GB/tree; delta UNKNOWN (derived) | Yes, with private worktree/index/status root | Sparse does not include ignored dependency roots; closure, self-links, compiled guards, and lane-root churn checks remain mandatory | Git move/repair survives; sparse state must be worktree-specific and links relative |\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/research.md:87:No COW or sandbox benchmark was run. “Fractional disk” and “faster setup” are hypotheses that must report initial apparent bytes, unique blocks after a representative write, setup wall time, mount/teardown time, and concurrent six-lane behavior separately from the existing 1.6 GB/149.7-second baseline.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/research.md:117:| Shallow clone alone | Truncates history but leaves the dense working tree that contributes to the 1.6 GB checkout | [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-clone] | 1, 3 |\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/research.md:150:1. Provision six registered sparse-plus-partial lanes at concurrency 3. Record setup wall time, materialized bytes, unique object bytes, lazy fetch count/latency, and comparison with the packet’s 16.5/149.7-second and 1.6 GB baseline.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/iterations/iteration-003.md:9:1. Reconciled the two earlier passes with the packet’s measured baseline. Six lanes at concurrency 3 took 16.5 seconds without worktrees and 149.7 seconds with worktrees; the packet records 1.6 GB of checked-out files per tree and approximately 9.6 GB for six trees, with the object store shared [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:98-110; specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/handover.md:39-44]. The raw wall-time delta is 133.2 seconds; no alternative mechanism was benchmarked in this lineage, so no fractional saving is claimed as observed.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/iterations/iteration-003.md:18:- Partial/blobless clone is complementary, not a substitute for sparse checkout. It reduces object transfer or lazy blob retention, while a dense checkout still materializes the snapshot. Shallow history similarly reduces history depth but not the dense working tree. Reference/shared clones reduce object duplication, but the packet already observes a shared object store for worktree lanes. These three mechanisms can preserve exact attribution only when wrapped in a private physical lane tree and Git metadata; none alone attacks the measured 1.6 GB checkout [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-clone; specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:105-106].\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/iterations/iteration-003.md:31:| Sparse checkout in a registered worktree | `git worktree add` plus sparse configuration; delta UNKNOWN | Selected tracked paths can be below the measured 1.6 GB/tree; delta UNKNOWN | Yes, if each lane keeps its own worktree/index/status root | Must include runtime and dynamic closure; ignored seven roots still use link/self-link rewrite; guards and churn work from the lane root | Git move/repair survives; sparse state must be worktree-specific, links relative, external dependencies checked |\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/iterations/iteration-003.md:33:| Shallow history | Clone/fetch depth setup; delta UNKNOWN | History/object bytes may fall, but dense working tree remains derived near the 1.6 GB checkout component | Yes only when paired with private lane tree; not an attribution optimization | Same dependency, self-link, guard, and churn behavior as the underlying worktree; history consumers may break | Registered worktree move survives; shallow-history assumptions travel with the repository |\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/iterations/iteration-003.md:44:If the dominant objective is the largest possible disk reduction, treat private-upper COW as a separate runtime feature, not a configuration toggle. It needs a new lane-view abstraction that owns Git admin/index, merged-root process routing, dependency reanchoring, containment, churn sampling, mount cleanup, and relocation. Its fraction-of-1.6-GB claim is a hypothesis until six-lane setup, apparent disk, changed-block disk, and failure-mode benchmarks pass.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/deep-research-strategy.md:15:Per-lineage git worktrees provide exact write isolation but cost roughly 1.6 GB of checkout and about 22 seconds of setup per lane in the six-lane measurement. Compare sparse and partial checkouts, shallow and reference clones, overlay or copy-on-write filesystems, one worktree with write redirection, per-lane sandbox directories with a shared object store, and other one-checkout designs against this repository.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/deep-research-dashboard.md:33:- [x] Where the 22 s / 1.6 GB goes: working-tree materialization + index; objects already shared.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/research.md:15:The measured per-lane cost — 1.6 GB of checkout and ~22 s of setup (six lanes at concurrency 3: 149.7 s with worktrees vs 16.5 s without) [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:105-106] — is almost entirely **working-tree materialization**: `git worktree add --detach … HEAD` writes all 82,393 tracked files and builds the lane index. The object store was never the cost — a linked worktree already shares it (this lane's `.git` entry is a 4 KB pointer). Every alternative must therefore be judged on how it materializes *bytes and index*, not objects.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/research.md:27:| Tree materialization + index | `git worktree add --detach … HEAD` [worktree-lifecycle.ts:592-593] | **dominant** — ~22 s, 1.6 GB |\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/research.md:40:| Full worktree (today) | ~22 s | 1.6 GB | Yes — private index + tree | — (baseline) | Yes |\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/prompts/iteration-001.md:12:Focus Area: cost decomposition of the measured 22 s / 1.6 GB per lane, and git-native reductions — sparse or partial checkouts, `--no-checkout` materialization, shallow and reference clones.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/iterations/iteration-002.md:20:1. **`git worktree add --no-checkout` + APFS `cp -Rc` is the strongest mechanism found.** Registration alone (gitdir under the shared `.git/worktrees/`, `.git` pointer file, no files materialized) costs tens of milliseconds; `cp -Rc` then clones the working tree as copy-on-write extents — a metadata operation per file, not a byte copy — putting realistic setup at roughly 1–4 s against the measured ~22 s [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/worktree-lifecycle.ts:592-593; specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:105]. Disk cost is ~0 at provisioning and grows only by the lane's own writes (CoW splits at write time). Six lanes cost ~0 + deltas instead of ~9.6 GB.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/iterations/iteration-002.md:32:7. **Index normalization is the one real engineering detail.** After `--no-checkout`, the lane index must be reconciled to the cloned bytes — either a sparse-checkout/`update-index --no-skip-worktree` pass that clears skip bits (a stat pass, not a byte copy) or seeding the lane's private index from the main checkout's index (the worktree index lives at `<commondir>/worktrees/<name>/index`, a stable-but-private layout). Even a degenerate full `git status` content-hash pass over the tree is seconds, not 22 s — and only stat-cache validation is needed after an index copy.\n=== decision-record worktree/ADR-003 ===\n3:description: \"The five decisions this packet freezes: preserve-by-default containment, baseline-targeted restore, a detached ephemeral worktree lane outside sk-git's numbered namespace, worktree isolation on by default with a per-attempt isolation tally, and a report-only watch of the shared checkout for isolated lanes whose process cwd stays in it.\"\n7:  - \"lineage worktree lane decision\"\n16:    recent_action: \"Appended ADR-005: the shared checkout is watched for isolated lanes whose cwd stays in it\"\n31:      - \"Whether the operator accepts an unnumbered runner-owned worktree lane — accepted; the lane is runner-owned and unnumbered (ADR-003), and ADR-004 turned its default on\"\n123:| 5 | **Open Horizons?** | PASS | It does not foreclose the worktree fix; it is the safe default underneath it |\n216:| 5 | **Open Horizons?** | PASS | Under per-lineage worktrees the baseline tree becomes cheap, since the worktree starts clean |\n234:## ADR-003: Lineage worktrees use a detached ephemeral lane, not sk-git's numbered allocator\n248:The structural fix is to run each lineage in its own worktree, so containment inside it is exact and the main checkout is never written by a lane. The repository's git safety rule says worktree creation goes through sk-git's allocator, and sk-git states the grammar: `worktrees/{NNN}-{slug}` branches with a per-namespace counter that is locked, seeded from a high-water mark, and never reused.\n250:That allocator is built for human task workspaces that live for days and are reaped after a merge. A fan-out creates one worktree per lineage per run — six lineages across a research program is dozens of worktrees a week, each existing for a few hours. Burning irreversible counter values on them makes the numbered namespace unreadable, and sk-git's reaper deliberately does not auto-clean anything outside the launch-wrapper lane, so the runner's worktrees would pile up as report-only entries.\n252:There is already a precedent for a non-allocator lane in the same repository: `.opencode/bin/worktree-session.sh` places each launch-wrapper session in an unnumbered `work/{runtime}/{slug}` branch with a `.worktrees/{runtime}-{slug}` directory, and sk-git documents that lane as distinct from the numbered one.\n256:- The ask-first worktree rule governs in-session decisions by an AI. A runner creating its own execution sandbox is closer to the launch wrapper: it is machinery the operator opted into by starting the run, not a workspace choice being made on their behalf.\n257:- A bare worktree lacks the gitignored `node_modules` and `dist`, which sk-git flags as a trap that makes the runtime generators crash or silently no-op.\n258:- The worktree must be removable without operator action, since a fan-out is non-interactive.\n264:**We chose**: The runner creates a detached ephemeral worktree per lineage, outside sk-git's numbered namespace, and removes it at lane teardown.\n266:**How it works**: One `git worktree add --detach` from HEAD per lineage, into a directory named by the runner's own prefix plus the run id and the lineage label, under the worktree base sk-git already resolves. The shared `node_modules` and `dist` are symlinked in, mirroring the launch wrapper. The target packet's uncommitted content is seeded in before dispatch so a lineage can read a spec that is not yet committed. Detached means no branch is created, so no name is allocated and nothing enters either counter. The runner sweeps its own prefix at startup so an interrupted run cleans up on the next one.\n274:| **Detached ephemeral worktree, runner-owned** | No branch and no counter consumed; self-cleaning; precedent in the launch-wrapper lane | Sits outside the sk-git allocator, which the repository rule names as the path for worktree creation | 8/10 |\n275:| Allocate through sk-git's numbered allocator | Follows the documented path exactly | Consumes never-reused counter values at fan-out rate; the reaper does not auto-clean the lane, so worktrees accumulate as report-only entries | 4/10 |\n276:| One shared worktree for the whole run | A single setup cost; still isolates from the main checkout | Sibling lineages write concurrently into one tree, which is the misattribution problem moved one level down rather than solved | 3/10 |\n277:| No worktree; rely on preserve mode alone | Zero new machinery | Attribution stays a guess, and the lane's own out-of-scope writes are never actually contained | 5/10 |\n286:- Containment inside a lineage worktree is exact: everything outside the lineage directory there really is that lineage's write.\n290:- One checkout per lineage of disk and inodes. Mitigation: worktrees share the object store, and teardown is part of lane settle.\n297:| An executor resolves a path relative to the original checkout and writes outside the worktree | H | Rewrite every path in the prompt pack and the dispatch flags; the kind with no directory flag runs with the worktree as its working directory |\n299:| Copy-back fails and artefacts are stranded | M | On copy-back failure the worktree is retained and its path is named on the failure event |\n309:| 3 | **Sufficient?** | PASS | Detached worktrees, symlinked dependencies, seeded packet content, copy-back and sweep; no new dependency |\n320:- `runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs`: worktree create, seed, path rewrite, copy-back, remove and startup sweep.\n","metadata":{"output":"     435 /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/fanout-attribution.md\n    6116 /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/synthesis.md\n    8591 /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/worktree-symlinks/synthesis.md\n    4568 /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/open-questions/synthesis.md\n    1922 /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/orchestration-summary.json\n   21632 total\n=== measurements: 1.6 GB / 22 s in packet ===\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/synthesis-v1.md:24:The packet records 16.5 seconds without worktrees versus 149.7 seconds with worktrees for six lanes at concurrency 3, and 1.6 GB of checked-out files per tree with a shared object store [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:98-110]. Alternative setup and disk deltas remain unmeasured.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/research.md:14:The packet’s observed baseline is 16.5 seconds for six lanes without worktrees versus 149.7 seconds with worktrees at concurrency 3, and 1.6 GB of checked-out files per tree; the object store is shared [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:98-110]. No alternative was benchmarked here, so every fractional cost claim is derived or inferred. The terminal loop stop is `maxIterationsReached` after three iterations; convergence mode was off.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/research.md:32:The packet reports 16.5 seconds without worktrees and 149.7 seconds with worktrees for six lanes at concurrency 3, a 133.2-second wall-time difference in that run. It reports 1.6 GB of checked-out files per tree and roughly 9.6 GB for six trees, while the Git object store is shared [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:98-110; specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/handover.md:39-44]. The packet’s decision record describes provisioning as the hot-path cost and worktrees as the structural isolation fix [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/decision-record.md:391-425].\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/research.md:58:| Sparse checkout in a registered worktree | `git worktree add` plus sparse configuration; delta UNKNOWN | Selected tracked paths can be below the measured 1.6 GB/tree; delta UNKNOWN (derived) | Yes, with private worktree/index/status root | Sparse does not include ignored dependency roots; closure, self-links, compiled guards, and lane-root churn checks remain mandatory | Git move/repair survives; sparse state must be worktree-specific and links relative |\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/research.md:87:No COW or sandbox benchmark was run. “Fractional disk” and “faster setup” are hypotheses that must report initial apparent bytes, unique blocks after a representative write, setup wall time, mount/teardown time, and concurrent six-lane behavior separately from the existing 1.6 GB/149.7-second baseline.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/research.md:117:| Shallow clone alone | Truncates history but leaves the dense working tree that contributes to the 1.6 GB checkout | [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-clone] | 1, 3 |\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/research.md:150:1. Provision six registered sparse-plus-partial lanes at concurrency 3. Record setup wall time, materialized bytes, unique object bytes, lazy fetch count/latency, and comparison with the packet’s 16.5/149.7-second and 1.6 GB baseline.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/iterations/iteration-003.md:9:1. Reconciled the two earlier passes with the packet’s measured baseline. Six lanes at concurrency 3 took 16.5 seconds without worktrees and 149.7 seconds with worktrees; the packet records 1.6 GB of checked-out files per tree and approximately 9.6 GB for six trees, with the object store shared [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:98-110; specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/handover.md:39-44]. The raw wall-time delta is 133.2 seconds; no alternative mechanism was benchmarked in this lineage, so no fractional saving is claimed as observed.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/iterations/iteration-003.md:18:- Partial/blobless clone is complementary, not a substitute for sparse checkout. It reduces object transfer or lazy blob retention, while a dense checkout still materializes the snapshot. Shallow history similarly reduces history depth but not the dense working tree. Reference/shared clones reduce object duplication, but the packet already observes a shared object store for worktree lanes. These three mechanisms can preserve exact attribution only when wrapped in a private physical lane tree and Git metadata; none alone attacks the measured 1.6 GB checkout [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-clone; specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:105-106].\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/iterations/iteration-003.md:31:| Sparse checkout in a registered worktree | `git worktree add` plus sparse configuration; delta UNKNOWN | Selected tracked paths can be below the measured 1.6 GB/tree; delta UNKNOWN | Yes, if each lane keeps its own worktree/index/status root | Must include runtime and dynamic closure; ignored seven roots still use link/self-link rewrite; guards and churn work from the lane root | Git move/repair survives; sparse state must be worktree-specific, links relative, external dependencies checked |\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/iterations/iteration-003.md:33:| Shallow history | Clone/fetch depth setup; delta UNKNOWN | History/object bytes may fall, but dense working tree remains derived near the 1.6 GB checkout component | Yes only when paired with private lane tree; not an attribution optimization | Same dependency, self-link, guard, and churn behavior as the underlying worktree; history consumers may break | Registered worktree move survives; shallow-history assumptions travel with the repository |\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/iterations/iteration-003.md:44:If the dominant objective is the largest possible disk reduction, treat private-upper COW as a separate runtime feature, not a configuration toggle. It needs a new lane-view abstraction that owns Git admin/index, merged-root process routing, dependency reanchoring, containment, churn sampling, mount cleanup, and relocation. Its fraction-of-1.6-GB claim is a hypothesis until six-lane setup, apparent disk, changed-block disk, and failure-mode benchmarks pass.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-luna/deep-research-strategy.md:15:Per-lineage git worktrees provide exact write isolation but cost roughly 1.6 GB of checkout and about 22 seconds of setup per lane in the six-lane measurement. Compare sparse and partial checkouts, shallow and reference clones, overlay or copy-on-write filesystems, one worktree with write redirection, per-lane sandbox directories with a shared object store, and other one-checkout designs against this repository.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/deep-research-dashboard.md:33:- [x] Where the 22 s / 1.6 GB goes: working-tree materialization + index; objects already shared.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/research.md:15:The measured per-lane cost — 1.6 GB of checkout and ~22 s of setup (six lanes at concurrency 3: 149.7 s with worktrees vs 16.5 s without) [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:105-106] — is almost entirely **working-tree materialization**: `git worktree add --detach … HEAD` writes all 82,393 tracked files and builds the lane index. The object store was never the cost — a linked worktree already shares it (this lane's `.git` entry is a 4 KB pointer). Every alternative must therefore be judged on how it materializes *bytes and index*, not objects.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/research.md:27:| Tree materialization + index | `git worktree add --detach … HEAD` [worktree-lifecycle.ts:592-593] | **dominant** — ~22 s, 1.6 GB |\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/research.md:40:| Full worktree (today) | ~22 s | 1.6 GB | Yes — private index + tree | — (baseline) | Yes |\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/prompts/iteration-001.md:12:Focus Area: cost decomposition of the measured 22 s / 1.6 GB per lane, and git-native reductions — sparse or partial checkouts, `--no-checkout` materialization, shallow and reference clones.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/iterations/iteration-002.md:20:1. **`git worktree add --no-checkout` + APFS `cp -Rc` is the strongest mechanism found.** Registration alone (gitdir under the shared `.git/worktrees/`, `.git` pointer file, no files materialized) costs tens of milliseconds; `cp -Rc` then clones the working tree as copy-on-write extents — a metadata operation per file, not a byte copy — putting realistic setup at roughly 1–4 s against the measured ~22 s [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/worktree-lifecycle.ts:592-593; specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/implementation-summary.md:105]. Disk cost is ~0 at provisioning and grows only by the lane's own writes (CoW splits at write time). Six lanes cost ~0 + deltas instead of ~9.6 GB.\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening/research/lineages/1789363775244-64o2qi-swe2/iterations/iteration-002.md:32:7. **Index normalization is the one real engineering detail.** After `--no-checkout`, the lane index must be reconciled to the cloned bytes — either a sparse-checkout/`update-index --no-skip-worktree` pass that clears skip bits (a stat pass, not a byte copy) or seeding the lane's private index from the main checkout's index (the worktree index lives at `<commondir>/worktrees/<name>/index`, a stable-but-private layout). Even a degenerate full `git status` content-hash pass over the tree is seconds, not 22 s — and only stat-cache validation is needed after an index copy.\n=== decision-record worktree/ADR-003 ===\n3:description: \"The five decisions this packet freezes: preserve-by-default containment, baseline-targeted restore, a detached ephemeral worktree lane outside sk-git's numbered namespace, worktree isolation on by default with a per-attempt isolation tally, and a report-only watch of the shared checkout for isolated lanes whose process cwd stays in it.\"\n7:  - \"lineage worktree lane decision\"\n16:    recent_action: \"Appended ADR-005: the shared checkout is watched for isolated lanes whose cwd stays in it\"\n31:      - \"Whether the operator accepts an unnumbered runner-owned worktree lane — accepted; the lane is runner-owned and unnumbered (ADR-003), and ADR-004 turned its default on\"\n123:| 5 | **Open Horizons?** | PASS | It does not foreclose the worktree fix; it is the safe default underneath it |\n216:| 5 | **Open Horizons?** | PASS | Under per-lineage worktrees the baseline tree becomes cheap, since the worktree starts clean |\n234:## ADR-003: Lineage worktrees use a detached ephemeral lane, not sk-git's numbered allocator\n248:The structural fix is to run each lineage in its own worktree, so containment inside it is exact and the main checkout is never written by a lane. The repository's git safety rule says worktree creation goes through sk-git's allocator, and sk-git states the grammar: `worktrees/{NNN}-{slug}` branches with a per-namespace counter that is locked, seeded from a high-water mark, and never reused.\n250:That allocator is built for human task workspaces that live for days and are reaped after a merge. A fan-out creates one worktree per lineage per run — six lineages across a research program is dozens of worktrees a week, each existing for a few hours. Burning irreversible counter values on them makes the numbered namespace unreadable, and sk-git's reaper deliberately does not auto-clean anything outside the launch-wrapper lane, so the runner's worktrees would pile up as report-only entries.\n252:There is already a precedent for a non-allocator lane in the same repository: `.opencode/bin/worktree-session.sh` places each launch-wrapper session in an unnumbered `work/{runtime}/{slug}` branch with a `.worktrees/{runtime}-{slug}` directory, and sk-git documents that lane as distinct from the numbered one.\n256:- The ask-first worktree rule governs in-session decisions by an AI. A runner creating its own execution sandbox is closer to the launch wrapper: it is machinery the operator opted into by starting the run, not a workspace choice being made on their behalf.\n257:- A bare worktree lacks the gitignored `node_modules` and `dist`, which sk-git flags as a trap that makes the runtime generators crash or silently no-op.\n258:- The worktree must be removable without operator action, since a fan-out is non-interactive.\n264:**We chose**: The runner creates a detached ephemeral worktree per lineage, outside sk-git's numbered namespace, and removes it at lane teardown.\n266:**How it works**: One `git worktree add --detach` from HEAD per lineage, into a directory named by the runner's own prefix plus the run id and the lineage label, under the worktree base sk-git already resolves. The shared `node_modules` and `dist` are symlinked in, mirroring the launch wrapper. The target packet's uncommitted content is seeded in before dispatch so a lineage can read a spec that is not yet committed. Detached means no branch is created, so no name is allocated and nothing enters either counter. The runner sweeps its own prefix at startup so an interrupted run cleans up on the next one.\n274:| **Detached ephemeral worktree, runner-owned** | No branch and no counter consumed; self-cleaning; precedent in the launch-wrapper lane | Sits outside the sk-git allocator, which the repository rule names as the path for worktree creation | 8/10 |\n275:| Allocate through sk-git's numbered allocator | Follows the documented path exactly | Consumes never-reused counter values at fan-out rate; the reaper does not auto-clean the lane, so worktrees accumulate as report-only entries | 4/10 |\n276:| One shared worktree for the whole run | A single setup cost; still isolates from the main checkout | Sibling lineages write concurrently into one tree, which is the misattribution problem moved one level down rather than solved | 3/10 |\n277:| No worktree; rely on preserve mode alone | Zero new machinery | Attribution stays a guess, and the lane's own out-of-scope writes are never actually contained | 5/10 |\n286:- Containment inside a lineage worktree is exact: everything outside the lineage directory there really is that lineage's write.\n290:- One checkout per lineage of disk and inodes. Mitigation: worktrees share the object store, and teardown is part of lane settle.\n297:| An executor resolves a path relative to the original checkout and writes outside the worktree | H | Rewrite every path in the prompt pack and the dispatch flags; the kind with no directory flag runs with the worktree as its working directory |\n299:| Copy-back fails and artefacts are stranded | M | On copy-back failure the worktree is retained and its path is named on the failure event |\n309:| 3 | **Sufficient?** | PASS | Detached worktrees, symlinked dependencies, seeded packet content, copy-back and sweep; no new dependency |\n320:- `runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs`: worktree create, seed, path rewrite, copy-back, remove and startup sweep.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"P=\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/fanout-1789366204116-kfp53e-attempt-1-glm/specs/system-deep-loop/045-fanout-write-containment-hardening\"; wc -c \"$P/research/fanout-attribution.md\" \"$P/research/synthesis.md\" \"$P/research/worktree-symlinks/synthesis.md\" \"$P/research/open-questions/synthesis.md\" \"$P/research/orchestration-summary.json\"; echo \"=== measurements: 1.6 GB / 22 s in packet ===\"; grep -rn \"1\\.6\" --include='*.md' \"$P\" | head -12; grep -rn \"22 *s\\|22-sec\\|22 second\\|~22\" --include='*.md' \"$P\" | head -8; echo \"=== decision-record worktree/ADR-003 ===\"; grep -n \"ADR-003\\|ADR-005\\|worktree\" \"$P/decision-record.md\" | head -25","time":{"start":1789366796914,"end":1789366796932}},"id":"prt_09e927742001k3FfBOp7AzAxCC","sessionID":"ses_f6176138cffeODZD3dhSK78dPo","messageID":"msg_09e921330001ibTk53Iex7eRnh"}}
